WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.275
Lesson 12.7.

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:05.155
Tax, regulation and compliance awareness.

00:00:05.155 --> 00:00:07.551
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:07.551 --> 00:00:10.232
to know which on-chain actions may have tax or

00:00:10.232 --> 00:00:13.483
legal consequences, keep the records to handle them, and

00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:15.480
know when to get professional help.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.070
Here's the plan.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.755
First, the key ideas.

00:00:19.755 --> 00:00:22.470
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:23.995
Then, your checklist.

00:00:23.995 --> 00:00:26.157
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:27.457 --> 00:00:30.032
This lesson is awareness, not advice.

00:00:30.032 --> 00:00:32.337
Rules differ by country and change.

00:00:32.337 --> 00:00:34.846
Use a crypto-aware tax professional and,

00:00:34.846 --> 00:00:36.982
for legal questions, a lawyer.

00:00:36.982 --> 00:00:39.773
First, Events that may be taxable in many

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:43.427
countries: selling crypto for cash, swapping one token

00:00:43.427 --> 00:00:47.547
for another, spending crypto, and receiving income (interest,

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:50.239
staking rewards, LP fees, airdrops).

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:54.989
Some treat borrowing, wrapping, LP deposits or bridging differently.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:55.864
Ask.

00:00:55.864 --> 00:00:59.219
Next, Cost basis: what you paid, including fees.

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:04.575
Methods (e.g. FIFO, specific identification) depend on local rules.

00:01:04.575 --> 00:01:08.003
Then, Records: every buy, sell, swap, transfer,

00:01:08.003 --> 00:01:11.003
fee and income event, with dates, amounts

00:01:11.003 --> 00:01:13.446
and prices (W1 records sheet).

00:01:13.446 --> 00:01:16.835
On-chain data can be exported, but self-transfers must

00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:19.846
be labelled or they may look like disposals.

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:24.784
Also, Regulation: use services legally available where you live;

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:29.569
don't use tools to get around geo-restrictions; never interact

00:01:29.569 --> 00:01:34.583
with sanctioned addresses or services; exchanges must run KYC/AML

00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:39.061
checks, and some DeFi front ends block certain regions.

00:01:39.061 --> 00:01:42.645
And finally, Reporting: some countries require reporting

00:01:42.645 --> 00:01:45.334
of foreign accounts or digital assets.

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:46.469
Ask your professional.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:50.285
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:50.285 --> 00:01:53.881
In one year: bought ETH, swapped half to USDC,

00:01:53.881 --> 00:01:58.166
lent USDC for interest, received an airdrop, bridged to

00:01:58.166 --> 00:02:02.062
an L2 and moved funds between your own wallets.

00:02:02.062 --> 00:02:05.393
For your tax professional, you'd prepare: the records

00:02:05.393 --> 00:02:09.650
sheet, labelled self-transfers (not disposals), interest and airdrop

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:13.281
income with dates and values, and exchange statements.

00:02:13.281 --> 00:02:15.296
They tell you what's taxable where you live.

00:02:16.596 --> 00:02:18.056
Here's your checklist.

00:02:18.056 --> 00:02:19.591
Do it now, for real.

00:02:19.591 --> 00:02:23.256
First, Records sheet complete, with self-transfers labelled.

00:02:23.256 --> 00:02:26.361
Next, Crypto-aware tax professional engaged.

00:02:26.361 --> 00:02:31.845
Then, only legally available services used; no geo-restriction workarounds.

00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:34.981
And finally, Sanctions awareness: never interact

00:02:34.981 --> 00:02:37.413
with sanctioned addresses or services.

00:02:38.713 --> 00:02:39.923
Question 1.

00:02:39.923 --> 00:02:42.223
Name three events that are often taxable.

00:02:46.523 --> 00:02:49.405
The answer: Any three: selling for cash,

00:02:49.405 --> 00:02:53.764
swapping tokens, spending crypto, receiving interest, staking

00:02:53.764 --> 00:02:55.803
rewards, LP fees or airdrops.

00:02:57.103 --> 00:02:58.348
Question 2.

00:02:58.348 --> 00:03:00.043
Why label self-transfers?

00:03:04.463 --> 00:03:06.486
The answer: So moves between your

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:08.986
own wallets aren't mistaken for disposals.

00:03:10.286 --> 00:03:11.556
Question 3.

00:03:11.556 --> 00:03:13.511
Who decides what's taxable for you?

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:20.320
The answer: Your local rules, applied

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.331
by a qualified tax professional.

00:03:23.631 --> 00:03:24.876
Let's recap.

00:03:24.876 --> 00:03:27.586
Events that may be taxable in many countries.

00:03:27.586 --> 00:03:30.526
Cost basis: what you paid, including fees.

00:03:30.526 --> 00:03:33.186
Records: every buy, sell, swap, transfer,

00:03:33.186 --> 00:03:35.576
fee and income event, with dates.

00:03:35.576 --> 00:03:39.351
Regulation: use services legally available where you live.

00:03:40.651 --> 00:03:43.166
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:43.166 --> 00:03:47.729
Next up, Lesson 12.8: Institutional-grade custody:

00:03:47.729 --> 00:03:50.861
MPC, custodians and policy engines.
